    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 1671273,
    "poster_id": 344076,
    "post_rendered": "In triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >,</span> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\"  width=\"11\" height=\"8\" > be the excircle opposite to <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/0/87082784a051c7525966d9819cc379329a30e030.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D, E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be the points where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\"  width=\"11\" height=\"8\" > is tangent to <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/b/6fbe007774b471bf8985d1da69d949014a746f87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" >,</span> and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" >,</span> respectively. The circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58cf2c01b32821ad23584e33cc96ba86963d7961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AEF$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > intersects line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"16\" >.</span> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > be the midpoint of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Prove that the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/4/8f41b10fc5f48da7fa1224c3121cb2d4157a46e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MPQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" > is tangent to <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\"  width=\"11\" height=\"8\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "In triangle $ABC$, let $\\omega$ be the excircle opposite to $A$. Let $D, E$ and $F$ be the points where $\\omega$ is tangent to $BC, CA$, and $AB$, respectively. The circle $AEF$ intersects line $BC$ at $P$ and $Q$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AD$. Prove that the circle $MPQ$ is tangent to $\\omega$.",
